{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdP1PGjfyNii5zD9myDzb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KvwLoxzCt7jvVqinsQ8oct","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/arena-ws.SfiLYf/SOLUTION.md","content":"# Solution\n\nTwo bugs in `lrucache/cache.py`, both in the recency bookkeeping.\n\n## 1. `get` did not count as a \"use\"\n\n`get` returned the value without reordering the `OrderedDict`, so a key's\nposition only ever reflected when it was *written*, never when it was *read*.\nThat makes the cache least-recently-**inserted** rather than least-recently-used:\nreading `\"a\"` then inserting a new key still evicted `\"a\"`. Fixed by calling\n`self._data.move_to_end(key)` on a successful (non-expired) lookup, matching the\ndocstring's \"Both `get` and `put` count as 'use'\".\n\n## 2. `put` evicted on updates to existing keys\n\nThe capacity check `if len(self._data) >= self.capacity` ran unconditionally, so\noverwriting a key that was *already present* first popped the LRU entry. Since\nthe following assignment reused the existing slot rather than adding one, the\ncache silently shrank and an unrelated key was lost — `put(\"y\", 3)` on a full\ncache destroyed `\"x\"`. Fixed by only evicting when the key is genuinely new\n(`if key not in self._data and len(...) >= self.capacity`).\n\n`python3 -m pytest -q` now passes (6 passed).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":411,"cache_read_input_tokens":24404,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":411,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"08d59dd4-b1ce-4bcf-8c96-50a81cec7993","uuid":"075dfc80-1337-4d17-98e3-1ef980200e66","timestamp":"2026-07-25T15:24:44.806Z","request_id":"req_011CdP1PG6EM9Ao75